  "enhanced_information": "The patient presents with a severe headache rated 10/10, associated with nausea, photophobia, and neck stiffness, which suggests possible meningeal irritation. Given the acute nature of the headache and the presence of neck stiffness, there is a high suspicion for meningitis or other serious intracranial pathology. While the patient has a history of migraines, the current headache is described as worse than any previous episodes, warranting further investigation to rule out secondary causes. A lumbar puncture is indicated to assess for infection, especially since the fundoscopic examination is normal, indicating no signs of increased intracranial pressure. Although a CT head may be considered to exclude hemorrhage, the presence of neck stiffness and the acute presentation strongly support the need for a lumbar puncture to evaluate for meningitis. Therefore, the next best step in management is a lumbar puncture.",
